# The one scene that deliberately misuses kaya: an app thread that stops
# taking its occurrences must be REPORTED (DESIGN.md's threading model).
# WHY TWO CLICKS: the consumer cursor advances BEFORE a record reaches
# the guest, so a handler blocking on an empty ring looks idle, and the
# second click is what makes work PENDING. THE REPORT IS ASSERTED ONLY
# OF AN ABSENCE THAT NEVER ENDS, because the margin for a bounded one
# GROWS WITH THE MACHINE (docs/traps.md, 2542ms on a loaded emulator).
expect label#0 "ready"

# IT GOES AWAY AND IT COMES BACK: the queued click is taken, so this is
# a stall and not a death and nothing was dropped.
click button#0
click button#1
expect label#0 "pinged"

# AND THE WATCHDOG TAKES IT BACK. Without this line the watchdog
# reported a stall on every healthy run in five of the languages — it
# counted occurrences into the transport against those the CORE handed
# over, so every guest reading the ring directly looked stalled
# (docs/traps.md). A diagnostic that fires when it should not is read as
# evidence, so both directions are asserted.
expect_no_stall

# THEN THE ONE THAT NEVER COMES BACK: the pending click stays pending,
# so the assertion can take as long as it likes. THE PAUSE KEEPS THE TWO
# HALVES APART, and expect_no_stall above is what proves the first
# cleared.
settle 300
click button#2
click button#1
expect_stall
# NOTHING FOLLOWS, and nothing can: the app thread never returns. That
# the leg still reports a verdict is the other half — the harness runs
# on its own thread and asks the MAIN thread to exit.
